          %p This entry is about the book
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ronnie+Brown"}> Ronnie Brown
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Philip+Higgins"} Philip Higgins
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rafael+Sivera"} Rafael Sivera
              %p
                %br/
              %p
                %strong Nonabelian Algebraic Topology: filtered spaces, crossed complexes, cubical homotopy groupoids
              %p
                %br/
              %p
                Tracts in Mathematics
                %strong 15
              %p European Mathematical Society (2011)
              %p ISBN 978-3-03719-083-8
              %p
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.4171/083"} doi:10.4171/083
              %p
                %a{:href => "http://groupoids.org.uk/nonab-a-t.html"} webpage
          %p
            This treats
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+topology"} algebraic topology
            using tools of
            = succeed "-theory:" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} strict ∞-groupoid
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homological+algebra"} homological algebra
            use of
            = succeed "es" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complex"} chain complex
            = succeed "es," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/crossed+complex"} crossed complex
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+groupoid"} fundamental groupoid
            and its strict
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher categorical
            generalizations to the cubical
            %span.newWikiWord
              fundamental omega-groupoid
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/fundamental+omega-groupoid"} ?
            of a filtered space over the bare
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+group"} homotopy group
          %p
            One of the main motivations for the development of Nonabelian Algebraic Topology was the observation that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Seifert-van+Kampen+theorem"} Seifert-van Kampen theorem
            is most naturally understood as being not about
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+group"} homotopy group
            %span.newWikiWord
              fundamental omega-groupoid
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/fundamental+omega-groupoid"} ?
            of a filtered space and may be generalized to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+homotopy+van+Kampen+theorem"} higher homotopy van Kampen theorem
            this way.
          %p
            The restriction to
            = succeed "s/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} strict ∞-groupoid
            = succeed "es" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/crossed+complex"} crossed complex
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            = succeed "-groupoids" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+topology"} algebraic topology
            in the guise of
            = succeed "es" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complex"} chain complex
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmic+cube"} cosmic cube
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher category theory
            the approach of
            %em Nonabelian algebraic topology
            used here is somewhere half way between homology and homotopy theory; it in this border area that traditional accounts seem to most lacking, and are unable to cope well with the nonabelian second relative homotopy group of a pair of spaces. The start of the new approach is to replace this by a homotopy double groupoid of a pair of spaces, which allows an
            = succeed "." do
              %em algebraic inverse to subdivision
