          %h2#history History
          %p
            Some comments from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ronnie+Brown"} Ronnie Brown
            himself:
          %blockquote
            %p
              I hope it is helpful to relate my experiences from the 1960s and later with
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonabelian+cohomology"} nonabelian cohomology
            %p
              In writing my book on
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topology"} topology
              in the 1960s, I got offended by having to make a detour to get the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+group"} fundamental group
              of the circle, and then was attracted by
              = succeed "\u2018s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paul+Olum"} Paul Olum
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mayer-Vietoris+sequence"} Mayer–Vietoris type sequence
              in the second paper below, and this enabled one to compute the fundamental group of, for example, a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wedge+product"} wedge
              of circles.
            %p
              (I use an MV sequence in
              %em Topology and Groupoids
              in connection with pullbacks of
              = succeed "s.)" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covering+space"} covering space
            %p
              So I decided to use this account for the book, thus giving students the advantage, it seemed, of an introduction to
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology"} cohomological
              ideas.
            %p The problem was that the account when written in detail came to 30 pages (or maybe 40) and when looked at in the cold light of day seemed incredibly boring (a full account is different from Olum’s research account).
            %p
              I was at the time looking for exercises and came across
              = succeed "\u2018" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Philip+Higgins"} Philip Higgins
              = succeed "s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presentation"} presentation
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonabelian+cohomology"} nonabelian cohomology
              stuff and moreover, when souped up to the
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+groupoid"} fundamental groupoid
                on a set of base points
              , gave results not reachable by the MV sequence; for example you could not with the MV sequence deduce the
              %em precise calculation
              of the fundamental group of a union of two open sets whose intersection had say 150 path components. (This anomaly is also significant, in illustrating the limitations of exact sequences.)
            %p
              So I decided to switch to an exposition of groupoids in 1-dimensional
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
              (also spurred by a meeting with
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/George+Mackey"} George Mackey
              in 1967 where he told me of his work on ergodic groupoids, which is now seen as a preliminary to
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+geometry"} Noncommutative Geometry
            %p
              It occurred to me that if one could come to the groupoid idea from two distinct directions, then there was likely to be more in this than met the eye. At the same time, an examination of the proof of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/van+Kampen+theorem"} van Kampen theorem
              for groupoids, suggested that the theorem should have an extension to all dimensions, if one could define homotopy gadgets with the right properties. Another stimulus was the proof (used in the book) by
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Frank+Adams"} Frank Adams
              (circulated in handwritten lecture notes) of the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cellular+approximation+theorem"} cellular approximation theorem
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo &gt;
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \gt 1
            %p
              It took 9 years to find such a gadget in dimension
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Philip+Higgins"} Philip Higgins
            %p
              It seemed to me unfortunate that this work aroused the opposition, for reasons never explained to me, of Frank Adams, who told people the whole programme was “ridiculous”. His opinion became the opposite only when I told him (1985?) of the extension to the non simply connected case of the Blakers-Massey description of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 3
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_3
              of a triad, using the nonabelian tensor product (work with
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jean-Louis+Loday"} Jean-Louis Loday
            %p
              The higher order van Kampen theorems, and the often nonabelian calculations which result, have not been obtained by cohomological methods, but only by working directly with structures appropriate to the geometry of higher homotopies, i.e. forms of strict
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-fold+category"} multiple groupoids
              = succeed "," do
                %span.newWikiWord
                  Philip Hall
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Philip+Hall"} ?
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimit"} colimit
              type theorems in algebraic (and geometric?) topology – no algebraic or geometric topology text (except mine!) mentions the higher order van Kampen work with Philip Higgins.
            %p
              You can also see this restriction in the contrast between the unsymmetrical, choice laden, definition of the second relative homotopy group, with its compositions in one direction (recall the limitations of “Lineland” described in “Flatland”) and the definition of the fundamental
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+groupoid"} double groupoid
              of a pointed pair of spaces
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ρ
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho_2(X,A)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
              directions. This contrast gets more significant in higher dimensions.
            %p
              For all these reasons, my inclination is to look for the applications of the “appropriate” (whatever that is!) structures rather than cohomology with coefficients in such structures, where lots of detail is likely to get lost. Also, in making calculations it is convenient to work with strict algebraic structures, where the notion of colimit is more comprehensible. Even there, it has been a problem to make say colimit calculations with
              = succeed "s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/crossed+module"} crossed module
              %span.newWikiWord
                Chris Wensley
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Chris+Wensley"} ?
              listed below.
            %p These results could not have been obtained without the intuitions on multiple compositions easily allowed by a cubical approach.
            %p
              One of the key observations for this programme was that one could define a strict homotopy double groupoid for a
              = succeed "," do
                %em pointed pair of spaces
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Whitehead"} Whitehead
            %p
              The natural extension of this observation is to construct a
              %span.newWikiWord
                strict cubical ∞-groupoid
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/strict+cubical+omega-groupoid"} ?
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ρ
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho X_*
              of a
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/filtered+space"} filtered space
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_*
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Π
                      %mo :
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi filtered
                      %mi spaces
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi crossed
                      %mi complexes
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi: (filtered spaces) \to (crossed complexes)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ρ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho
              a colimit theorem for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Π
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi
              one can shortcut
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singular+homology"} singular homology
              = succeed "s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+realisation"} geometric realisation
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+set"} simplicial
              or
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cubical+set"} cubical
              sets. These ideas generalise of course to
              = succeed "s" do
                %span.newWikiWord
                  multifiltered space
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/multifiltered+space"} ?
              = succeed "-cubes" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              %em must
              work with a kind of bare topological space, and so have little handle on which to construct invariants, except say by first taking a singular complex, or using multipaths.
            %p
              The main idea of the
              = succeed "s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+homotopy+van+Kampen+Theorem"} higher homotopy van Kampen Theorem
            %p
              An indication of a beginnings of a Čech type approach to nonabelian cohomology using groupoids and crossed complexes is given in the new book, Chapter 12. This has not been developed in terms of
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf+theory"} sheaf theory
            %p
              Another big gap in comparison with traditional algebraic topology is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intersection+theory"} intersection theory
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poincare+duality"} Poincare duality
            %p
              An obvious gap is also that of extending
              = succeed "\u2018s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck"} Grothendieck
          %h2#contents_2 Contents
          %h3#i_1_and_2dimensional_results I 1 and 2-dimensional results
